Deino × Brightkite Logistics: discovery
Wednesday, September 30, 2026 at 08:01 · 25 min

Attendees
---------
Lena Okafor, Shreyas, Marcus Webb, Hannah Pike

Notes
-----
Decisions

• The pilot will cover one Brightkite region with about 70 technicians for 60 days, instead of all three regions at once.
• The pilot proposal will be priced on the enterprise tier ($60 per technician per month) because Brightkite needs Okta SSO and SCIM.
• The pilot will be paid (no free pilot), with the pilot fee credited against the first year if Brightkite signs an annual contract.
• No real Brightkite data goes into Deino until Hannah signs off on the security review; an anonymised sample sent via Brightkite's secure file share is allowed before that.
• Deino will run a tailored demo for Brightkite's dispatchers on Wednesday 7 October at 2pm Brightkite time, focused on drag-and-drop rescheduling.

Key facts

• How big is Brightkite's field operation? About 220 field technicians and 14 dispatchers across three regions (East, Central and West).
• When does Brightkite's DispatchPro licence end? 31 January 2027.
• How many jobs does each Brightkite dispatcher handle per day? Around 60 jobs a day.
• What share of Brightkite's jobs get rescheduled? About 11% are rescheduled at least once (probably higher in reality).
• Who makes the buying decision at Brightkite, and when is budget approved? Marcus decides together with the CFO (Hannah effectively has a security veto); budget approval happens in November.
• What are Hannah's security requirements? SSO through Okta, a SOC 2 report, US data residency, and a summary of the most recent penetration test.
• What is Deino's SOC 2 status? Deino has a SOC 2 Type I report; the Type II observation window starts on 1 November. Hannah accepts Type I for a pilot but needs the Type II timeline in writing for a full contract.
• Where is Deino's data hosted? AWS in the US: primary region us-east-2 (Ohio), backups in us-west-2.
• What price tier would Brightkite be on? The enterprise tier at $60 per technician per month (standard plan is $45), because they need SSO and SCIM.
• What does Carlos want from a new dispatch tool? Drag-and-drop rescheduling that warns about conflicts before confirming, and a phone view of which technicians are running late.
• Does Deino offer route optimisation or a NetSuite integration? No. Route optimisation is not on the roadmap this quarter, and there is no native NetSuite integration (only an API and CSV exports).

